# nFPM configuration for the `fermix-desktop` package.
#
# One download installs Fermix on a Linux desktop: the window, the engine, and
# the toolkit the window links. The design is
# docs/design/SINGLE_PACKAGE_AMENDMENT.md, sections 3 and 4.
#
# Rendered by scripts/build_packages.sh, which fills every placeholder below and
# refuses to leave one behind. One rendered file produces both the deb and the
# rpm, so the two families cannot drift into two file lists.
#
# The version is the tag's, exactly: `version_schema: none` keeps nFPM from
# reshaping it, and `release` is left empty so the Debian version carries no
# revision. nFPM's rpm packager defaults an empty release to "1", so the rpm is
# fermix-desktop-<version>-1.<arch>.rpm while the deb is
# fermix-desktop_<version>_<arch>.deb with no revision. A desktop-only fix is
# published as <engine version>+<n>, which both dpkg and rpm order after the
# plain version, so the asymmetry costs nothing.
#
# **nFPM generates no dependencies.** Everything below is written by hand and
# proved afterwards: scripts/package_dependencies.py reads the NEEDED entries of
# every ELF in the staged tree and refuses a package that names a library
# neither carried in the private prefix nor covered by a declared relation, and
# refuses a declared relation no ELF in the package needs. The declaration and
# the machine's own answer are held equal in both directions.
name: fermix-desktop
arch: {{ARCH}}
platform: linux
version: "{{VERSION}}"
version_schema: none
release: ""
maintainer: Tezra <hello@fermix.ai>
description: |
  Fermix for Linux, the window and the engine.
  A native GTK4 and libadwaita client for the Fermix assistant: set it up, see
  whether it is running, change what it does, run its checks and read its log.
  It carries the engine your systemd runs and the toolkit the window draws
  with, so one install is the whole product.
homepage: https://fermix.ai
license: MIT
section: utils
priority: optional

# The relations, section 3.1.
#
# This package is the alternative to the engine-only `fermix`, not a layer on
# top of it, so the exact-version relation between the two is gone and three
# relations take its place. It **provides** the engine, so anything that names
# the engine by name is satisfied and both `dpkg -S /usr/bin/fermix` and
# `rpm -qf /usr/bin/fermix` answer with a package. It **conflicts** with it, so
# the two are mutually exclusive. On the deb side it also **replaces** it, which
# is what lets dpkg hand `/usr/bin/fermix` from one package to the other inside
# one transaction; `Replaces` alone forces nothing.
#
# The rpm side deliberately carries no such takeover relation. Converting a
# server's engine-only install into the desktop package on the next upgrade
# would pull a private toolkit onto a headless host, which is the opposite of
# what the split exists for.
#
# The floor is glibc, declared rather than discovered, so apt and dnf refuse on
# a host below it before anything unpacks and say what is missing. The toolkit
# is not declared at all in either family: the package carries its own, and a
# host relation on it would refuse to install on the oldest supported target,
# which is the one target the private runtime exists for.
#
# Everything else is section 4.1's host column, narrowed to what the toolkit
# that actually ships reaches for: the driver, the X11 wire-protocol clients,
# and the two frozen-SONAME libraries its own objects link.
#
# Six relations the amendment lists are deliberately absent, because the built
# toolkit needs none of them and scripts/package_dependencies.py refuses a
# relation no ELF justifies as firmly as it refuses a missing one. libdbus-1-3
# is not there because GLib speaks the D-Bus protocol itself rather than through
# libdbus; libdrm2, libgbm1 and libx11-xcb1 are the host driver's own
# dependencies and arrive with it; libzstd1 and liblzma5 are linked by nothing
# in the tree. A declaration nobody can justify is a promise about a user's
# machine that nothing checked.
#
# The three OpenGL rows are declared although no ELF in the package names
# them. GTK reaches OpenGL through libepoxy, which dlopens libGL.so.1,
# libEGL.so.1 and libGLESv2.so.2 rather than linking them, so a boundary read
# out of NEEDED entries misses all three and the first symptom on a host
# without one is a window that aborts. scripts/build_packages.sh carries them
# on its named exemption list for the same reason.
#
# The last rows of each list are not libraries the window links. Each is there
# because something the package carries reads a file the host owns, so
# scripts/package_dependencies.py is told by name that they have no NEEDED entry
# to justify them. The bundled GSettings backend is a client of the host's dconf
# service and of the host's schemas, and on a session with no Settings portal it
# is the only path the window has to the theme (amendment section 2.1). The
# private fontconfig is built with -Dbaseconfig-dir=/etc/fonts, so it reads the
# host's configuration and the host's fonts: the package owning that directory
# is a hard requirement, because without it the toolkit has no configuration at
# all.
#
# One relation on each list is not a library at all but a **program**, and it is
# the only one of its kind here. The engine stores a channel bot key through the
# host's secret service, and it does so by shelling out to `secret-tool` rather
# than by linking libsecret: the call is System.find_executable("secret-tool"),
# so the thing that must be present is an executable on PATH. A desktop with
# gnome-keyring running and org.freedesktop.secrets on the bus still refuses to
# save a key when that program is not installed, which is exactly what a Pop!_OS
# install reported, and no ELF in this package names it, so nothing derived from
# NEEDED entries would ever have caught it.
#
# The two families disagree about where it lives, so this relation is the first
# that is not the same shape on both sides. On deb the executables are split out
# of the library into libsecret-tools. On rpm there is no such split: the file
# belongs to libsecret itself, measured in almalinux:9 (libsecret-0.20.4-4.el9)
# and fedora:44 (libsecret-0.21.8.2-2.fc44). Naming the file capability rather
# than the package is what keeps that difference from becoming a guess, and it
# is also arch-clean, where a bare `libsecret` could be satisfied by the i686
# build both repositories carry.
#
# A font is a **recommendation** rather than a requirement, and the difference
# is deliberate. A host with no font installed draws no text, which is a bad
# window but a working install, and naming one family as a hard dependency would
# pull DejaVu onto a machine that has already made a different and perfectly
# good choice. Both apt and dnf install recommendations by default, so the
# ordinary install gets a font; the administrator who has arranged their own
# keeps it.
#
# A **keyring** is recommended for the same reason and with a sharper edge.
# secret-tool is only a client: it talks to whatever owns org.freedesktop.secrets
# on the session bus, and a desktop carrying neither a keyring nor any other
# secret service has nowhere to put a key however many programs are installed.
# So gnome-keyring is recommended in both families, and the ordinary install of
# a bare desktop gets one.
#
# It is deliberately NOT a dependency, and this is the case where the difference
# does real harm rather than merely being tidy. A KDE user's kwallet and a
# KeePassXC user's own daemon already provide org.freedesktop.secrets, and both
# satisfy the engine completely. A hard relation would install a second secret
# daemon beside a working one, on a machine that asked for neither, and two
# services racing for the same bus name is a worse outcome than the problem it
# would be solving. A recommendation reaches the person who has nothing and
# steps aside for the person who has already chosen.
overrides:
  deb:
    provides:
      - fermix (= {{VERSION}})
    conflicts:
      - fermix
    replaces:
      - fermix
    depends:
      - libc6 (>= 2.34)
      - libgcc-s1
      - libgl1
      - libegl1
      - libgles2
      - libx11-6
      - libxcb1
      - libxcb-render0
      - libxcb-shm0
      - libxext6
      - libxi6
      - libxcursor1
      - libxdamage1
      - libxfixes3
      - libxrandr2
      - libxinerama1
      - libxrender1
      - libxkbcommon0
      - zlib1g
      - libyaml-0-2
      - libcurl4
      - dconf-service
      - gsettings-desktop-schemas
      - fontconfig-config
      - libsecret-tools
    recommends:
      - fonts-dejavu-core
      - gnome-keyring
  rpm:
    provides:
      - fermix = {{VERSION}}
    conflicts:
      - fermix
    depends:
      - libc.so.6(GLIBC_2.34)(64bit)
      - libgcc_s.so.1()(64bit)
      - libGL.so.1()(64bit)
      - libEGL.so.1()(64bit)
      - libGLESv2.so.2()(64bit)
      - libX11.so.6()(64bit)
      - libxcb.so.1()(64bit)
      - libxcb-render.so.0()(64bit)
      - libxcb-shm.so.0()(64bit)
      - libXext.so.6()(64bit)
      - libXi.so.6()(64bit)
      - libXcursor.so.1()(64bit)
      - libXdamage.so.1()(64bit)
      - libXfixes.so.3()(64bit)
      - libXrandr.so.2()(64bit)
      - libXinerama.so.1()(64bit)
      - libXrender.so.1()(64bit)
      - libxkbcommon.so.0()(64bit)
      - libz.so.1()(64bit)
      - libyaml-0.so.2()(64bit)
      - libcurl.so.4()(64bit)
      - dconf
      - gsettings-desktop-schemas
      - fontconfig
      - /usr/bin/secret-tool
    recommends:
      - dejavu-sans-fonts
      - gnome-keyring

contents:
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The engine's files are not listed here, and that is the point.
  #
  # The engine artifact carries its own `contents:` block as
  # `nfpm-contents.yaml`, already resolved, and that block is the single author
  # of where the engine's files go and what mode they carry.
  # scripts/build_packages.sh splices it in below the marker on the next line,
  # rewriting each `src:` to the verified staging directory and refusing any
  # entry whose destination is not under a path the engine owns or collides with
  # one of this repository's own.
  #
  # Restating those paths here was the alternative and is refused: an engine
  # release that adds a packaged file, one more shell completion say, would then
  # turn the automated version bump red until a person hand-edited this file,
  # which is exactly the manual stitching one package exists to remove.
  #
  # The marker below appears exactly once and the build refuses otherwise.
  # >>> engine contents, spliced from the verified archive

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The window, and the private toolkit runtime it links.
  #
  # The application ELF lives inside the private prefix and resolves its
  # libraries through the RUNPATH $ORIGIN/../lib, which is the directory below
  # it. /usr/bin/fermix-desktop is a symbolic link to it and never a wrapper
  # script: a wrapper would mean the process the desktop sees is not the one the
  # launcher, StartupWMClass and the activation unit all name, and it would put
  # a library directory into an environment every child inherits.
  #
  # The three trees are copied wholesale because none can be named file by
  # file: the runtime is some hundreds of shared objects, and its caches and
  # compiled schemas are generated at build time against this exact absolute
  # prefix. scripts/check_private_runtime.sh reads the staged tree back and
  # holds every one of them to that.
  #
  # The runtime installs exactly three directories under the prefix -- lib,
  # libexec and share -- and every one of them has to be named here. libexec was
  # missing until 2026-09-20, and with it went two programs that are started by
  # absolute path rather than linked: gio-launch-desktop, which GLib spawns for
  # every g_app_info_launch_default_for_uri, and dconf-service. Nothing noticed,
  # because a program spawned by path is not a NEEDED entry and so appears in no
  # relation and no link check; the only symptom was that opening a URL from the
  # window silently did nothing on an installed machine, which the owner
  # reported as the browser never opening. scripts/check_runtime_complete.sh now
  # holds the built package to the runtime's own file list so that a directory
  # added to the runtime and forgotten here is a refusal rather than a feature
  # that quietly stops working.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/lib/fermix-desktop/bin/fermix-desktop
    dst: /usr/lib/fermix-desktop/bin/fermix-desktop
    file_info:
      mode: 0755

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/lib/fermix-desktop/lib
    dst: /usr/lib/fermix-desktop/lib
    type: tree

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/lib/fermix-desktop/libexec
    dst: /usr/lib/fermix-desktop/libexec
    type: tree

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/lib/fermix-desktop/share
    dst: /usr/lib/fermix-desktop/share
    type: tree

  # For a symbolic link nFPM reads `src` as the target and `dst` as the link,
  # which is the one entry in this file where the two read backwards, so the
  # link is named first and the target second.
  -
    dst: /usr/bin/fermix-desktop
    src: ../lib/fermix-desktop/bin/fermix-desktop
    type: symlink

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # What the desktop reads.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/applications/io.tezra.Fermix.desktop
    dst: /usr/share/applications/io.tezra.Fermix.desktop
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/metainfo/io.tezra.Fermix.metainfo.xml
    dst: /usr/share/metainfo/io.tezra.Fermix.metainfo.xml
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/dbus-1/services/io.tezra.Fermix.service
    dst: /usr/share/dbus-1/services/io.tezra.Fermix.service
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/lib/systemd/user/app-io.tezra.Fermix.service
    dst: /usr/lib/systemd/user/app-io.tezra.Fermix.service
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  # Nine rasters, because SVG support is explicitly optional in the icon theme
  # specification, plus the scalable icon and its symbolic pair.
  #
  # This list names one file at a time, so a size added to ICON_SIZES in
  # build_packages.sh is STAGED but not PACKAGED until it is also named here.
  # That is the shape of the libexec defect: the staging tree was right, the
  # package was short, and nothing failed. scripts/check_runtime_complete.sh is
  # what catches it, by holding the built package against the tree it was built
  # from rather than against this list.
  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/16x16/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/16x16/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/22x22/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/22x22/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/24x24/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/24x24/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/32x32/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/32x32/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/48x48/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/48x48/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/64x64/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/64x64/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/128x128/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/128x128/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/512x512/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/512x512/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.png
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.svg
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/io.tezra.Fermix.svg
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/symbolic/apps/io.tezra.Fermix-symbolic.svg
    dst: /usr/share/icons/hicolor/symbolic/apps/io.tezra.Fermix-symbolic.svg
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # What this build was.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The installed build identity. The application compares the id here with the
  # one compiled into it and says nothing at all when either is absent, so the
  # two are written from one value at build time rather than by hand.
  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/fermix-desktop/build.json
    dst: /usr/share/fermix-desktop/build.json
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  # Generated from packaging/runtime/RUNTIME.lock.json, one stanza per bundled
  # component with its version, its source and its licence in full.
  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/doc/fermix-desktop/copyright
    dst: /usr/share/doc/fermix-desktop/copyright
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

  # The lock file plus the sha256 of every file the private runtime installed,
  # which is what makes the licensing obligation answerable from an installed
  # machine rather than only from this repository.
  - src: {{STAGE}}/usr/share/doc/fermix-desktop/runtime-manifest.json
    dst: /usr/share/doc/fermix-desktop/runtime-manifest.json
    file_info:
      mode: 0644

# Each of these is assembled at build time from two fragments, the engine's
# taken byte for byte out of the engine artifact and the desktop's from
# packaging/scripts/. scripts/assemble_maintainer.sh does the assembly and
# proves the engine half is unchanged.
scripts:
  postinstall: {{POSTINSTALL}}
  postremove: {{POSTREMOVE}}
